  "note": "PLAN CONTEXT: every tool's plan_id accepts the plan's NAME as the user says it (e.g. 'apartment_01.dwg') - pass the user's words through. In multi-plan chats, when the user switches plans or it is ambiguous which plan they mean, SAY which plan you are acting on (replies name it too) or check with list_my_plans - never silently reuse the previous plan. Meters, plan coordinates (+y = north). Walls/openings/stairs have stable ids; FIXTURE, POOL, PARKING and PERGOLA ids are list positions and SHIFT after removals - call get_plan_structure again after editing. The wall between two rooms is the wall whose 'rooms' lists both room ids; likewise an opening's 'rooms' lists what it connects ('the door between rooms 5 and 8' = the opening whose rooms has both; 'close all openings in room 5 except to room 8' = remove openings whose rooms contain 5 but not 8). A pool whose overlapsWalls lists wall ids is cutting through those walls - after rotating or moving a pool, re-check and move it clear (move pool with a new center) unless the user wants it there. NAMING ROOMS: name_room sets a room's display label (any language, e.g. 'Bathroom 2' or 'מטבח'; empty name clears it; bare numbers rejected - they mean room ids). The name shows on the plan, overrides any drawing-extracted label, and works in later requests ('the kitchen'). ANCHORS: annotations are named marks both you and the user can point at - points (A, B...), measured lines (M1...), regions (R1...), and FLOOR TEXT (T1...). write_text_on_floor writes words on the floor at a point - any language, works where no room was detected; compute the point from geometry, e.g. 'north of room 8' = past room 8's north wall). Reference them by name; create your own to SHOW the user something with add_marker and tell them to look. The user sees them on the plan with the same names.",
